  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe problem requires finding a step function h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0, 1] that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085, which measures the overlap between h(x) and 1 - h(x + k) for any shift k. The constraints are \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 and 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1. \n\nThe current approach reinterprets the problem as maximizing the minimum auto-correlation of h, since C\u2085 = max_k (1 - \u222b h(x)h(x + k) dx). This reframing allows leveraging techniques from signal processing and optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nDefine h as a discrete vector of size n_points, with 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n_points / 2. The objective is to minimize:\n$$C\u2085 = \\max_k \\left(1 - \\sum_{i=0}^{n_points} h[i] h[(i+k) \\mod n_points] \\cdot dx \\right)$$\nwhere dx = 2.0 / n_points. \n\nWe aim to maximize the minimum value of the auto-correlation \u2211 h[i] h[i +k] dx across all k. This is a non-convex optimization problem due to the quadratic terms.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialize\n- Use initial_h_values if available; else, create a binary vector with n_points / 2 ones.\n- Set n_points to 95 if initial_h_values is provided, otherwise use a reasonable default.\n\nb. Define objective and gradient\n- Compute auto-correlation using FFT for efficiency.\n- Identify the shift(s) where auto-correlation is minimized.\n- Compute the gradient based on these shifts.\n\nc. Optimization loop\n- Use L-BFGS with constraints handling.\n- After each update, project h onto the feasible set: clip to [0, 1] and rescale to maintain sum(h) = n_points / 2.\n- Track the best solution and terminate early if time budget exhausted.\n\nd. Post-processing\n- Validate the solution with the original evaluator.\n- Ensure constraints are satisfied.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n- Start from initial_h_values and perturbed versions.\n- Use multiple random seeds for initialization.\n- Vary n_points between 95, 190, and 285 to explore resolution effects.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- FFT cross-correlation ensures O(n log n) per iteration.\n- Expect ~1000 iterations within 1100s for n_points=200.\n- Handle numerical issues by validating constraints post-update.\n\n6. Validation and return\n\n- Verify \u2211h = n_points / 2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.\n- Recompute C\u2085 with the evaluator.\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points).",
